<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">March of Ancona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March of Ancona was a frontier province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on the Adriatic coast, roughly the modern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>. It took shape under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries and was recovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> under Innocent III, after which the Papal States governed it as a province until its annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860. Its towns, led by Ancona, enjoyed wide self-government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-of-ancona.jpg" alt="March of Ancona" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Jolle at Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_March_of_Ancona.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
